WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.380
Reflection lets .NET code inspect assemblies, types, members, attributes, metadata, and runtime objects.

2
00:00:07.480 --> 00:00:13.240
It is a powerful capability because code can reason about other code while the application is running.

3
00:00:13.340 --> 00:00:17.980
That flexibility supports many common frameworks and developer workflows.

4
00:00:18.260 --> 00:00:26.900
Reflection can also create instances, invoke methods, access properties, read attributes, and support dynamic behavior.

5
00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:41.160
Serializers, dependency injection containers, object mappers, plugin systems, testing tools, command dispatchers, validation frameworks, and diagnostic utilities may all depend on reflection in some way.

6
00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:51.040
Reflection becomes security-sensitive when runtime behavior depends on data from users, partners, configuration, plugins, files, or external systems.

7
00:00:51.140 --> 00:00:59.940
If a runtime name can influence which type is activated or which method is invoked, then a data value has become part of the execution path.

8
00:01:00.220 --> 00:01:07.660
The secure baseline starts with a simple review question: what type or member is allowed, who decides, and what can happen after invocation?

9
00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:18.480
That question should be answered before reflection touches application state, sensitive data, authorization decisions, filesystem access, service providers, or administrative capabilities.

10
00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:21.160
Reflection is not automatically unsafe.

11
00:01:21.260 --> 00:01:23.820
The risk comes from unreviewed flexibility.

12
00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:33.040
A typed design, explicit map, trusted contract, or narrow registry is often easier to reason about than unrestricted runtime discovery.

13
00:01:33.140 --> 00:01:40.900
Reflection should be intentional, documented, and bounded by the same validation and authorization expectations as the rest of the application.
